`,(0,d.jsx)(b3.p,{children:"I wanted a way to set up a homelab without spending a lot of money (if any). My daily driver is a Macbook Air and when studying for my CompTIA A+ exam, Windows 10/11 settings came up frequently. I wanted to be able to play around with various settings, maybe try out some basic networking without going to the trouble of buying a new device."}),`
`,(0,d.jsx)(b3.h2,{children:"How?"}),`
`,(0,d.jsx)(b3.p,{children:"My setup:"}),`
`,(0,d.jsxs)(b3.ul,{children:[`
`,(0,d.jsx)(b3.li,{children:"Model Name:	MacBook Air"}),`
`,(0,d.jsx)(b3.li,{children:"Chip:	Apple M1 (arm64)"}),`
`,(0,d.jsx)(b3.li,{children:"Total Number of Cores:	8 (4 performance and 4 efficiency)"}),`
`,(0,d.jsx)(b3.li,{children:"Memory:	16 GB"}),`
`]}),`
`,(0,d.jsxs)(b3.p,{children:["After noodling around on Reddit I initially tried ",(0,d.jsx)(b3.a,{href:"https://mac.getutm.app/",children:"UTM"}),". Supposedly it runs ARM64 OSs on Apple Silicon at 'near native speeds'. I found it was a bit buggy - had issues with black screens and installing the Windows image. I chose to uninstall it and went with ",(0,d.jsx)(b3.a,{href:"https://www.vmware.com/products/desktop-hypervisor/workstation-and-fusion",children:"VMWare Fusion Pro"})," instead, free for personal use. Seemed a better choice because it comes up a lot in job ads."]}),`
`,(0,d.jsxs)(b3.p,{children:["I downloaded the Windows 11 image from the ",(0,d.jsx)(b3.a,{href:"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/windowsinsider/preview-windows",children:"Windows Insider Preview Builds"}),", after registering for the program."]}),`
`,(0,d.jsx)(b3.p,{children:"From there it was pretty straightforward - setting up a virtual machine (VM) with the Windows 11 ISO I downloaded earlier, and just following the wizard. I chose UEFI Secure Boot in case the process was buggy like with UTM, but it wasn't."}),`
`,(0,d.jsxs)(b3.p,{children:["I also set up an Ubuntu Server VM with the ISO from ",(0,d.jsx)(b3.a,{href:"https://ubuntu.com/download/server/arm",children:"Canonical"}),". A few months back I asked Claude for a tutorial to set up a server and I thought it would be 'fun' to set up an Ubuntu Server VM now."]}),`
`,(0,d.jsx)(b3.h2,{children:"Now what?"}),`
`,(0,d.jsx)(b3.p,{children:"I ran Windows Update and did the same with Defender to make sure I'm up to date on the latest patches/security definitions. So far I've tweaked Remote Desktop settings to use my Mac host and Windows App to 'remotely' connect to my Windows 11 VM."}),`
`,(0,d.jsx)(b3.p,{children:"I've also connected to my Ubuntu server from Terminal and Powershell through SSH. Most of the time this works fine, occasionally a few hiccups with IP address/connection issues."}),`
`,(0,d.jsxs)(b3.ul,{children:[`
`,(0,d.jsxs)(b3.li,{children:[`
`,(0,d.jsxs)(b3.p,{children:[(0,d.jsx)(b3.strong,{children:"Learning Curve"}),": I've had a couple of patchy issues connecting to my Windows VM from my Mac host and vice versa. Eventually I learned to set up an inbound firewall rule through Windows Defender Firewall. I allowed ICMPv4 (for ping), set the IP address to my Mac host and also disabled Stealth mode on my Mac firewall."]}),`
`]}),`
`,(0,d.jsxs)(b3.li,{children:[`
`,(0,d.jsxs)(b3.p,{children:[(0,d.jsx)(b3.strong,{children:"Challenges"}),": Driver issues with installing Windows 11 image - I uninstalled the UTM Guest Tools and had to adjust my Mac Accessibility settings. VMWare/Broadcom website a bit tricky to navigate."]}),`
`]}),`
